## The Coalition Calculation Framework

Successful multi-party election trading requires a framework that traditional binary-market thinking does not provide. First, traders must map the ideological space to understand which parties can plausibly cooperate. Second, they must calculate threshold effects, since parties that fail to clear electoral minimums redistribute their votes in ways that cascade through coalition mathematics. Third, they must price the gap between plurality and power, that spread between winning the most seats and actually governing.

The Israeli markets expiring within 24 hours offer a concentrated lesson in these dynamics. Whether Netanyahu returns to power depends not just on Likud's performance but on the complex interaction of a dozen smaller parties, threshold mathematics, and post-election negotiation dynamics that polling cannot capture but markets must price. This is prediction market pricing at its most sophisticated, a real-time calculation of political probability that incorporates information streams no poll could ever aggregate.

